= Roadmap

There are no dates. Each milestone has *exit criteria*. A milestone is done when its gallery documents open in Word with no repair prompt, pass both validators, and match their golden snapshots.

== M0: Foundations

- \[x\] Repository setup: `uv`, `ruff`, `pyright --strict`, `pytest`, GitHub Actions (Windows, Linux, macOS), license. CONTRIBUTING is still to do.
- \[x\] OPC package writer: content types, relationships, deterministic zip.
- \[x\] Minimal part writers: document, styles (docDefaults, the four default styles, user styles), settings, core and app props, fontTable, theme (own default theme).
- \[x\] Validation pipeline: lxml + ECMA-376 Transitional XSDs (`schemas/`); `tools/ooxml-validate` (.NET OpenXmlValidator).
- \[x\] Golden-test harness (canonical XML snapshots in `tests/golden/expected/`).
- \[x\] First version of the property registry: 15 run and 14 paragraph properties, with schema-order tables checked against `wml.xsd`.
- \[x\] *Spike:* 5 representative documents in the proposed syntax. See the findings.
- *Exit:* the Python API produces a valid document with styled paragraphs. This is met locally: the golden cases pass both validators and open in Word 365 with the expected formatting. CI has not run yet because the repository has no GitHub remote.

== M1: Core language (roughly "Pandoc parity")

- \[x\] Parser for all modes, lossless CST, typed AST, source spans, diagnostics renderer (`wordgenx.syntax`; rules in design §3.22). Checked by property-based fuzzing (Hypothesis) and a syntax tour (`examples/syntax-tour.wgx`).
- \[x\] CLI `wordgenx check` and `wordgenx parse` (`--tree ast|cst`).
- \[x\] Lowering AST -> IR (`wordgenx.lower`, design §3.23): inline sugar, formatting commands, `\p`, `\align`, option type-checking, units and colours (incl. theme `lighter`/`darker`), smart punctuation.
- \[x\] Style sheets (`.wgxs` and `\styles{}`): defaults, paragraph, character and linked styles, theme references, modify/replace, multilevel numbering definitions with style-linked levels (`numbering.xml`). Table styles are parsed but deferred to M2.
- \[x\] Headings, paragraphs, lists with real multilevel numbering (separate lists restart; `\list[Name, continue]`, `restart=N`).
- \[x\] Single-section page setup, page and column breaks, hyperlinks.
- \[x\] Inline images (PNG, JPEG, GIF, BMP) and tables: pipe and block forms, widths, horizontal and vertical merges, shading, header rows, row height, nested tables (borders via `Table Grid`; custom table styles in M2).
- \[x\] `\include`, `\use` (style sheets and macro files), `\let`, `\define` (macros with `#param`), `\settings`, `\document`, `\meta`.
- \[x\] Command registry with argument kinds (markup, string, raw, math); the comment, whitespace and flanking rules from the spike.
- \[x\] Standard macro library (`std:callout`, `std:signature`), packaged with the compiler.
- \[x\] Trial: the spike's design specification ported to v0.2 (`examples/design-spec/`, findings in its `NOTES.md`).
- \[x\] CLI: `wordgenx build` (and `check` runs the full compile).
- *Exit:* a real 20-page document can be authored entirely in wordgenx. Language grammar v0.1 is frozen. _Status:_ the 4-page specification trial builds cleanly, and its remaining gaps (headers and footers, TOC, cross-references, captions, footnotes, sections) are M2 items. The grammar has been stable through M1 (v0.2 plus the §3.22–3.23 rules); freezing it is the last step.

== M2: Word structure (where wordgenx goes beyond Pandoc)

- \[x\] Multiple sections (orientation, size, margins, columns, break types); headers and footers (first, even, linked or not); page-number formats and restarts; fields (`\pagenum`, `\numpages`, `\field`, `\date`); tab stops.
- \[x\] Bookmarks; cross-references with pre-computed text (`@label`, `\ref`, `\pageref`); captions (`\caption`, `\figure`, code-block captions); `\seq` counters; TOC and lists of figures/tables with pre-rendered entries.
- \[x\] Footnotes and endnotes (`\footnote`, `\endnote`, labels and `NOTEREF`, numbering format, start, restart and position settings).
- \[x\] Full tables: merges, borders, shading (theme colours), repeat headers, cell margins, look flags, table styles with conditional formatting (header row, last row, first/last column, banded rows/columns, corner cells).
- \[x\] Templates: compile into a `.dotx` or `.docx` (styles, numbering, theme, page setup, headers and footers, body content, other parts); write `.dotx` templates.
- \[x\] Heading numbering, legal numbering, tab stops, paragraph borders (sides, between, bar) and shading.
- \[x\] Document properties: core, custom (`\meta[custom.NAME = ...]`, merged with a template's) and `DOCPROPERTY`; general `\field`.
- \[x\] Sweep: document protection, mirror margins, line numbering, section vertical alignment, decimal table columns, chapter-numbered captions, references in headers and footers, per-cell margins and text direction, more run and paragraph properties (scale, kerning, emboss, imprint, outline, shadow, suppress line numbers and hyphenation), and the escape hatches (`\raw[ooxml]`, `ooxml.*` properties). Moved out of M2: list (numbering) styles (later), columns of different widths (M4).
- *Exit:* wordgenx's own design docs are rebuilt in wordgenx with a corporate-style template and look professionally typeset in Word. _Status: met._ `docs/book/design.wgx` builds the eight design documents into one 38-page book with the `docs/book/wordgenx.dotx` template (itself written in wordgenx): cover page, contents, numbered chapters with running heads, styled tables with repeated header rows, boxed code, notes, front matter with roman page numbers. It builds without diagnostics, passes both validators and opens cleanly in Word. The chapters are converted from the Markdown sources by `tools/md2wgx.py`, and the test suite keeps them in step.

== M3: Technical writing

Steps, in order:

1. \[x\] *Math* (LaTeX subset to OMML, `wordgenx.math`). Inline `\m{…}`, display `$$…$$` and `\math[…]{…}`. Fractions, roots, scripts, big operators with limits, delimiters (`\left…\right`), functions and `\lim`, accents and bars, matrices and `cases`, `aligned` rows with `&` alignment, text and font styles (`\mathbf`, `\mathbb`, …), Greek letters and the common symbol set. Unsupported macros are errors at their position. OMML is written the way Word writes it (the reference survey settled Q9). A numbered display equation is an `m:eqArr` with Word's own `#` number. Labelled equations are numbered, optionally per chapter, and `@eq:x` prints "(3.2)".
2. \[x\] *Code highlighting* (Pygments): token runs in generated, themeable character styles (`Code Keyword`, …), `line-numbers`, captions as now.
3. \[x\] *Citations and bibliography*: BibTeX or CSL-JSON data, CSL styles through citeproc-py, `\cite`/`\textcite` linked to entries, `\printbibliography` in a `Bibliography` style. Bundled styles: Harvard, APA, IEEE, Chicago author-date.
4. \[x\] *Index*: `\ix{term!sub}` as `XE` fields, `\index[columns=2]` as an `INDEX` field with pre-rendered entries.
5. \[x\] *Images and embedding*: SVG (with a PNG fallback, as Word stores it: `fallback=` or a grey placeholder), `\embed-docx` (altChunk, merged by Word on open).
6. \[x\] *Tooling*: `wordgenx fmt` (CST-based formatter, `--check`, `--diff`; §3.21), `--emit-ir` (the IR as JSON, one-way).
- *Exit:* a thesis-style document with math, citations, code and figures. _Status: met._ `examples/thesis/thesis.wgx` is a 13-page MSc thesis in five included chapter files with its own style sheet: title page, abstract, contents, lists of figures, tables and listings, front matter with roman page numbers, chapters with running heads, numbered equations referenced per chapter ("(2–1)"), APA citations and references, highlighted code listings, an SVG and a PNG figure, tables with repeated header rows, footnotes and a two-column index. It builds without diagnostics, passes both validators, and after Word updates its fields the contents, lists, index and references are all right. Building it found two bugs, both fixed: the equation bookmark covered the parentheses, so Word's update of a reference showed "((2.1))", and a chapter separator other than "." was set as math, spacing an en dash like a minus sign.

== M4: Advanced layout

Steps, in order (the small ones first; the drawing steps share one positioning model):

1. \[x\] *Page decoration*: page borders on sections (`borders=`), the page colour (`\document[page-color=…]`), borders and shading on runs.
2. \[x\] *Custom themes*: `theme [Name] { accent1: …; major-font: …; }` in a style sheet sets the theme's colours and fonts (wordgenx's theme part or the template's), so `theme.accent1` and `theme.major` mean the document's own choices. Tints and shades are computed as Word computes them (HSL luminance).
3. \[x\] *Floating images*: `\image[wrap=…, x=…, y=…]` as `wp:anchor` (square, tight, top-and-bottom, behind, in front), positioned against the page, margin, column or paragraph; crop, rotation and a border on any image.
4. \[x\] *Text boxes and watermarks*: `\textbox` (a `wps` shape with markup inside, inline or floating, with fill and border), `\watermark` (text or a picture behind every page of a section, in the header).
5. \[x\] *Frames and floating tables*: drop caps (`w:framePr`; other text frames are postponed), floating tables (`w:tblpPr`), columns of different widths.
6. \[x\] *Right-to-left and complex scripts*: right-to-left paragraphs, tables, runs and sections; complex-script and East Asian fonts and languages; bold and italic for complex scripts; vertical sections.

Basic shapes (lines, arrows, rectangles) come only if they stay small; see Postponed features.

- *Exit:* a magazine-style report: a cover page with a full-bleed picture, floating figures with text around them, a pull quote in a text box, a drop cap, a "DRAFT" watermark and its own theme. _Status: met._ `examples/magazine/magazine.wgx` is a three-page review: a full-bleed cover behind white Georgia titles; a two-column feature with a page-wide picture at the top, a drop cap in the heading font, a pull quote floating across the columns, a tightly wrapped picture, a floating table, a sidebar box and an SVG figure; a running head, a page frame and a DRAFT watermark, all in its own Harbour theme. It builds without diagnostics, passes both validators and lays out in Word as intended. Building it changed two things: percentage widths in a section with columns are now of the column, and a drop cap skips floating objects anchored at the start of its paragraph.

== M5: Collaboration & forms

Steps, in order. Each stays simple: a part that turns out intrusive (new parts, Word-version extensions, changes all over the writer) goes to Postponed features instead.

1. \[x\] *Settings*: `\settings[track-changes]` (Word's Track Changes on for edits made later in Word) and `hyphenation=auto` (left over from M4).
2. \[x\] *Comments*: `\comment[author=…, initials=…, date=…, text={…}]{commented text}`: the range, the reference mark and `comments.xml`; markup in the comment; a point comment without a body. Replies and the resolved state are left out.
3. \[x\] *Content controls*: `\control[type=…, tag=…, title=…, lock=…]`, inline or around blocks: plain and rich text, dropdown and combo box, date, checkbox; placeholder text; with the existing form protection.
4. \[x\] *Picture controls* (an image, or an empty frame). Repeating sections are postponed (Word 2013's `w15` markup and a section that repeats are more than they give).
- *Exit:* a fillable onboarding form: every field type, form protection, a reviewer's comments, Track Changes on. _Status: met._ `examples/forms/onboarding.wgx` is a one-page form protected for filling in: text, dropdown, combo, date, picture, rich-text and checkbox fields with placeholders, a locked field, a reviewer's comments (one with markup) and Track Changes on. It builds without diagnostics and passes both validators; in Word all fourteen controls read back with their types, filling a field, a dropdown and a checkbox works under protection, the comments show in the margin, and once unprotected, edits are tracked.

Tracked revisions in the source and `wordgenx diff` redlines are postponed; data binding stays "Later".

== M6: Import (`docx → wordgenx`)

The goal is a *reliable import of the significant part of a document and its formatting*, not a round trip: some data is inevitably stripped or approximated, and the import says what (no silent loss). `wordgenx import doc.docx` writes a folder: `doc.wgx`, a style sheet, the pictures, and a report. The reader (`.docx` → IR, behind `wordgenx inspect`) exists since M1; the work is mostly the other half, printing IR as readable source, plus filling the reader's gaps. The thinking behind it is in the decompiler note.

Steps, in order. Each stays simple; what turns out intrusive is reported as lost and listed in Postponed features.

1. \[x\] *Measure and fill the reader's gaps.* Build a small public corpus of Word-authored test documents (made through Word itself, so they are ours to commit) and run `inspect` on them and, locally, on the user's real test document. Count what the reader drops, by construct, and teach it the most frequent ones first, starting with what the writer already supports: sections with headers and footers, notes, comments, fields (TOC, REF, SEQ, PAGE), bookmarks, numbering, tables with merges, floating pictures, text boxes, content controls. _Status:_ `tools/corpus/make-corpus.ps1` drives Word to build six documents in `tests/corpus` (text, tables, references, sections, drawings, review), and `tests/ooxml/test_corpus.py` checks what each holds is read, that the only losses are the expected ones, and that the IR read back writes a valid package. The reader now reads notes, comments, block fields (the TOC), table styles with their conditions, table and cell borders and margins, paragraph and run borders and shading, floating pictures with crop, border and rotation, text boxes, drop caps, EMF/WMF previews of embedded objects, page borders and section columns. On the private test document the fallbacks went from 557 to 48 (text effects, orphan bookmark ends, emphasis marks, one embedded object), and what is read writes a package both validators accept.
2. *Print canonical source (L1).* IR → `.wgx` with commands only: `\p[style=…]`, direct formatting as `\font[…]`, `\table`/`\row`/`\cell`, `\section`, `\header`/`\footer`, `\footnote`, `\image` (pictures extracted), fields as their commands or `\field`. Escaping, and units snapped to what a person writes (1cm, 12pt). Anything not printable becomes a `% import:` comment and a line in the report. The check: the printed source compiles without errors and has the original's text.
3. *Styles.* A style sheet with the styles the document uses (and their bases), printing only what differs from wordgenx's built-ins; numbering definitions; the theme's colours and fonts; document defaults. Alternative mode: keep the original's styles, theme, numbering and headers exactly by writing them as a body-less template the source compiles into (`\document[template=…]`).
4. *Clean up and sugar (L2).* Merge Word's run soup into runs that differ in effective formatting, drop proofing and revision-id noise, and use the language's own forms where the result compiles to the same thing: `=` headings, `-` and `1.` lists, `*strong*`/`_emph_`, pipe tables, `{#label}` and `@label` from bookmarks and REF fields, `\toc`, captions from SEQ fields.
5. *The oracle.* `wordgenx import --check` compiles the imported source and compares it with the original: text, block structure, paragraph styles and effective character formatting, reported as a share of paragraphs that match and a list of differences. Round-trip tests on the examples (`.wgx → .docx → import → .docx`) keep it honest.
- *Exit:* the user's real test document imports with its text complete, its structure (headings, lists, tables, sections, notes) and most of its formatting, the report lists what was lost, and the recompiled document is checked in Word. That document is private: it is only read locally, and neither it nor its imported output is committed or published; committed tests use the public corpus.

Tooling (language server, editor support, `watch`, PDF export and `finalize`, a website) waits until the language has settled: see tooling ideas.

== Later / candidates

Native charts, native Word bibliography, content-control data binding, embedded fonts, building blocks. Postponed indefinitely: see Postponed features.

